<p>The World Health Organization declared the Ebola disease outbreak in the Democratic Republic of the Congo and Uganda a public health emergency of international concern, saying the outbreak requires a coordinated international response but does not meet the criteria for a pandemic emergency. The outbreak is caused by Bundibugyo virus disease, a rarer Ebola species, and WHO said the decision followed confirmed cases in both countries, documented international spread and a high risk to countries sharing land borders with the DRC. WHO said that, as of Saturday, DRC authorities had reported eight laboratory-confirmed cases, 246 suspected cases and 80 suspected deaths in Ituri province, including in the Bunia, Rwampara and Mongbwalu health zones. Uganda reported two laboratory-confirmed cases in Kampala, including one death, among people who had traveled from the DRC. The DRC also reported a confirmed case in Kinshasa in a person returning from Ituri. WHO said clusters of deaths and the rising number of suspected cases in Ituri point to a potentially much larger outbreak than current detection shows, with significant local and regional risk of spread. The agency advised countries not to close international borders and recommended cross-border screening, screening on main internal roads and activation of national emergency-management mechanisms.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/wF5DHm4iw-biuNT2IgJBAf_hcGnX16yauytdI0VqfccarDo6uXb3NV7SLGHixMxAVbJ9OMWJukJBQPUGxTR3GGxiJ9-B71jPL5zdurtuCU_c' alt='A staff member of the CBCA Virunga Hospital checks a visitor’s temperature using a contactless infrared thermometer before allowing her access to the hospital in Goma, Democratic Republic of the Congo, on May 17, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Treatment gap: WHO called the outbreak “extraordinary” because response teams cannot rely on a Bundibugyo-specific vaccine or established treatment protocol, unlike better-equipped Ebola-Zaire responses.</li><li>North Kivu watch: WHO said suspected cases had also been reported in North Kivu, one of Congo’s most populous provinces and a neighbor of Ituri.</li><li>Security constraints: Reports citing laboratory confirmation on Sunday said a case was confirmed in Goma, a major eastern DRC city controlled by the Rwanda-backed M23 militia.</li><li>Health workers: Early reports from Mongbwalu included deaths among health workers; investigations followed across the Mongbwalu, Rwampara and Bunia health zones.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>World Health Organization: WHO Director-General Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus thanked the DRC and Uganda for their commitment to “necessary and vigorous actions” and for frank risk assessments that allowed the global determination. (<a href='https://www.who.int/news/item/17-05-2026-epidemic-of-ebola-disease-in-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-and-uganda-determined-a-public-health-emergency-of-international-concern'>WHO</a>)</li><li>Nigeria Centre for Disease Control and Prevention: Nigeria’s NCDC said there was no confirmed Ebola case in the country and that it had intensified surveillance at ports of entry. (<a href='https://www.bellanaija.com/2026/05/who-declares-ebola-outbreak-drc-uganda-global-health-emergency-2026/'>BellaNaija</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/what-do-we-know-about-the-ebola-outbreak-in-congo-and-uganda'>What do we know about the Ebola outbreak in Congo and Uganda?</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/africa/article/3353866/ebola-outbreak-dr-congo-uganda-declared-international-health-emergency'>Ebola outbreak in DR Congo, Uganda declared an international health emergency</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/aa55ba2b-8476-4718-8d28-5f60df6ff19c'>WHO declares global health emergency on Ebola</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/2000924/who-declares-ebola-outbreak-in-dr-congo-uganda-an-emergency-of-internation